 "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}], "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "Whoops! Something important is missing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time...", "type": "text"}]}, "type": "feedback"}, {"type": "learning_skills", "contents": [{"content": "Reading", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Writing", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Speaking", "type": "skill"}]}, {"type": "post_instructions", "contents": [{"content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}]}], "type": "DWS", "template_override": 1}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "id": 0}, {"id": 1, "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"text": "", "id": 0, "time": "0", "image": "engenn11le04ob02re2img01.jpg"}, {"text": "If you want to keep powering your smartphone, pick up one of the new thermoelectric generators. If this technology works, you can power your cellphone someday soon by using your body heat. Forget using a clumsy charger, the thermoelectric generator can actually convert heat into energy - electrical energy! Imagine wearing this small generator as a wristband. It produces power (about 40 milliwatts of it) by using the slight difference in temperature between your skin and the air surrounding it when you have it on. Soon, you will be able to start charging devices that don't take too much energy, like cellphones, with one.", "id": 1, "time": "100", "image": "engenn11le04ob02re2img01.jpg"}, {"text": "Remember watching people use those handheld medical scanners on Star Trek? If it is to be believed, they are a step closer to reality thanks to a new breakthrough in technology. Scientists have developed a way to use the technology behind full-body security scanners. The waves from these are also capable of detecting biological changes, like an increase in the flow of blood around a tumor. The researchers report finding they can be used to make better compact medical scanning gadgets. If this happens smaller, cheaper, easier to use devices are on their way!", "id": 2, "time": "32200", "image": "engenn11le04ob02re2img02.jpg"}, {"text": "If the new round of universal translators is any indication, they are not ready to use yet. Try using a device that is not word perfect. Start up a casual conversation by insulting\nsomeone. Not exactly what you wanted to do? Well, if you employ the machine at this time, that is precisely what it ends up doing. Most of the tests have gone roughly as well as this.\nIf you want a machine to recognize voices, it is much harder to make one than originally\nthought, it seems.\n<br><br>\nSome innovations are coming, some faster than others. Scientists\nnever think about giving up. They are always interested in moving forward.", "id": 3, "time": "62500", "image": "engenn11le04ob02re2img03.jpg"}], "audiosrc": "engenn11le04ob02re2aud01", "coverimg": "engenn11le04ob02re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "_data": [], "type": "audio", "template_override": 1}]}]}